     {:id
      "decision-criterion/language/library/record-compatibility-event",
      :text
      "Decision criterion interface/language/library/record-compatibility-event: Classify API/profile changes under semantic compatibility rules; breaking changes carry explicit migration and feasible shim records with source/version identity. Reject: Reject silent breaking changes, stale compatibility evidence and unchecked/semantics-changing shims; preserve justified infeasibility when no safe rewrite exists. Required outputs: Compatibility event/version record, migration/shim or infeasibility artifact and preserved/broken-client fixtures.",
      :field "Closing evidence:",
      :before-value
      "Actual supported platform API fixtures, unsafe audits and compatibility/stability report; STD16-STD17 fixtures distinguish target/build/runtime-host facts, all clock/random kinds, process command/args/environment/stdio/timeout/working-directory policy, MMIO widths/alignment/volatile ordering, interrupt effects/reentrancy, DMA ownership/coherence/completion and clock-domain synchronization. STD20 release evidence compares source, behavior, effects, capabilities, diagnostics, artifact schemas, profiles and fixtures; exercises experimental opt-in/internal-import denial, deprecation/replacement/removal schedules and governed breaking changes; and binds signed provenance to the actual release. Platform target and product-release outcomes retain their separate scope. Retain the STD20 per-release report/evidence assembly and exact release binding outcome. Reuse governance baseline/dimension comparison and PKG10/PKG12 provenance/signing producers as dependencies rather than reimplementing them. Every release still requires the appropriate compatibility and signed provenance reports; a compatibility classification or signature alone does not assemble those release-bound artifacts or confer governance approval. Reject the following false substitutes: A report can identify an incompatible change; its existence is not approval to release it. A valid signature over another artifact/release does not satisfy STD20008. A signed provenance record cannot replace old/new conformance comparison or the complete compatibility dimensions. STD17 library/device communication requires synchronization proof or checked adapters. For actual physical HDL crossings, the adapter must satisfy B9's source/destination clock/reset/latency/metastability and declared synchronizer/FIFO/handshake/proof or hardware-policy waiver rules. Software/device adapters and simulator APIs are not automatically physical HDL crossings; keep their checked-adapter outcome and capabilities distinct. Reject the following false substitutes: A clock-domain spelling or register declaration is not a synchronization check. A passing HDL CDC gate does not prove a standard device API invokes the checked adapter. A generic unsafe island is not a B9 hardware audit waiver, and a simulator does not confer live-device authority.",
      :before-sha256
      "c1a829b8db8557a77bbaedee27b972019d10f3670a5e3a3df39901135910dd43",
      :append
      "Decision criterion interface/language/library/record-compatibility-event: Classify API/profile changes under semantic compatibility rules; breaking changes carry explicit migration and feasible shim records with source/version identity. Reject: Reject silent breaking changes, stale compatibility evidence and unchecked/semantics-changing shims; preserve justified infeasibility when no safe rewrite exists. Required outputs: Compatibility event/version record, migration/shim or infeasibility artifact and preserved/broken-client fixtures.",
      :source-file-sha256
      "dc4b07a5dcf8a06d58cf5612d3a275c21a41af5e851a81b771263cc0c610708e"},
